---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const scaleCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'side length a', min: 1, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: 'm' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'perimeter (4a)', expr: '4*a', unit: 'm' },
    { label: 'area (a²)', expr: 'a*a', unit: 'm²' },
    { label: 'volume (a³)', expr: 'a^3', unit: 'm³' },
    { label: 'volume ÷ area (a/6 surface law)', expr: 'a^3/(6*a*a)', unit: 'm' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/perimeter-area-volume">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Measurement answers a child's question — "how big is it?" — and the answer splits three
    ways depending on what "big" means. <strong>Perimeter</strong> is the distance around the
    edge: a length, measured in metres, the kind of bigness a fence cares about.
    <strong>Area</strong> is the amount of surface inside: measured in square metres, the kind
    of bigness a carpet or a coat of paint cares about. <strong>Volume</strong> is the amount
    of space filled: measured in cubic metres (or litres, where 1 L = 1,000 cm³), the kind of
    bigness a water tank or a lung cares about. The units are not decoration; they are the
    logic. A square metre literally is a square one metre on a side, so a 4 m × 3 m floor
    holds twelve of them — which is why area comes out as a <em>product</em> of lengths and
    why every area formula you will meet is a clever way of counting unit squares without
    laying them by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deeper story is how the three measures behave when things change size. Double a
    cube's side and its <strong>perimeter doubles</strong>, its <strong>area quadruples</strong>,
    its <strong>volume octuples</strong>. That mismatch — length ×1, area ×4, volume ×8 — is
    the <em>square–cube law</em>, and it quietly runs biology, engineering, cooking and the
    price of everything. First the formulas, then the law that falls out of them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="drag the side and watch three measures drift apart — volume always wins" widget="sliderLab" config={scaleCfg} />

  <h2>Perimeter and area: the workhorse formulas</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Perimeter</strong> is honest addition: walk the boundary and add the sides. For a
    rectangle of length l and width w, that is P = 2(l + w). <strong>Area</strong> of the same
    rectangle is A = l × w, the unit-square counting argument above; a square of side a has
    area a² — the very reason squaring is called squaring. A <strong>triangle</strong> is half
    a rectangle in disguise: cut any triangle's bounding rectangle along the apex and the
    two leftover triangles rearrange, giving A = ½ × base × height — and the height means
    perpendicular height, not the slanted side, the classic exam trap. A
    <strong>parallelogram</strong> shears into a rectangle of the same base and height
    (A = bh); a <strong>trapezium</strong> with parallel sides a and b is the average of the
    two parallel sides times the height, A = ½(a + b)h, because it is the average of two
    parallelogram-like slices. Every one of these is provable by cutting and rearranging —
    which is why they are worth knowing <em>cold</em>: they are not conventions but theorems
    about counting squares.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example: a triangular garden bed has base 6 m and perpendicular height 2.5 m —
    area ½ × 6 × 2.5 = 7.5 m². Fencing it costs per metre of <em>perimeter</em>; mulching it
    costs per square metre of <em>area</em>. Two different questions, two different measures,
    two different prices — conflating them is how DIY budgets die.
  </p>

  <h2>Circles, arcs and sectors: the special number π</h2>
  <p>
    Every circle shares one magic ratio: the circumference divided by the diameter is the
    same for <em>all</em> circles, everywhere — π ≈ 3.14159, an irrational number whose digits
    never repeat or end. So <strong>C = 2πr</strong> (or πd), and the area is
    <strong>A = πr²</strong> — which you can see by slicing a disc into thin wedges and
    standing them in a row: the row's length is half the circumference (πr) and its height is
    the radius, so the "rectangle" has area πr × r. <strong>Arcs and sectors</strong> are
    fractions of the whole: a sector with angle θ° is θ/360 of the circle, so its arc is
    (θ/360) × 2πr and its area (θ/360) × πr². The fraction is the whole trick — the sector is
    a pie slice, and pie slices are shares. Real-world case: a 30 cm pizza cut into 8 has
    sector angle 45°, arc length (45/360) × π × 15 ≈ 5.9 cm of crust, and area
    (45/360) × π × 15² ≈ 88 cm². And the classic decision: is one 40 cm pizza more pizza
    than two 30 cm ones? One 40 cm holds π × 20² ≈ 1,257 cm², while two 30 cm hold
    2 × π × 15² ≈ 1,414 cm² — the smaller pair wins, because area grows with the square of
    the radius, not the diameter. Do the arithmetic every time; pizza intuition is
    notoriously bad.
  </p>

  <h2>Surface area and volume: nets of 3-D solids</h2>
  <p>
    In three dimensions, <strong>volume</strong> counts unit cubes and <strong>surface
    area</strong> is the total area of the boundary — best imagined by unfolding the solid
    into a flat <em>net</em>: a cereal box is six rectangles, so its surface area is the sum
    and its volume is length × width × height. A <strong>prism</strong> (any shape extruded
    along a length) has V = (area of cross-section) × length: a cylinder is a circular
    prism, <strong>V = πr²h</strong>, with surface area 2πr² + 2πrh (two discs plus a rolled
    rectangle of height h and width 2πr — check the net). The pointed cousins:
    <strong>cone</strong> V = ⅓πr²h — exactly a third of the cylinder it sits in, as
    Cavalieri-style slicing proves — and <strong>sphere</strong> A = 4πr², V = (4/3)πr³.
    Notice the sphere's formula implies A = 4 × (the circle of the same radius): the sphere's
    surface is exactly four of its great-circle shadows, a result Archimedes considered his
    greatest discovery and asked to have carved on his tomb.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "surface area and volume grow together." They do not — and
    the gap between them is one of the most consequential ratios in science.
  </p>

  <h2>Compound shapes: build and subtract</h2>
  <p>
    Real shapes are compound: an L-shaped room, a running track, a window with a semicircular
    top. The universal strategy is <strong>decompose and combine</strong> — split the shape
    into rectangles, triangles and sectors whose formulas you know, then add the areas; for
    holes and cut-outs, compute the big shape and <strong>subtract</strong>. A running track
    is a rectangle flanked by two semicircles (together one full circle): a 100 m straight and
    36.5 m radius bends give area 100 × 73 + π × 36.5² ≈ 7,300 + 4,186 ≈ 11,486 m². The same
    logic in reverse gives <strong>parts of shapes</strong>: knowing the whole and one part,
    the other follows. This add-and-subtract habit is also dimensional analysis in miniature —
    keeping careful track of which measure (m, m², m³) each term belongs to catches most
    errors before they happen, because you can never add a length to an area.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unit conversion is dimensional analysis in the wild, and the square–cube law hides
    inside it: converting <em>lengths</em> multiplies by the linear factor, but areas by
    its <em>square</em> and volumes by its <em>cube</em>. 1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m² = 100² =
    10,000 cm² — a hundred times more than the naive answer — and 1 m³ = 100³ = one
    million cm³ (which is exactly why 1 litre = 1,000 cm³ and a cubic metre holds 1,000
    litres). Worked compound example, top to bottom: a window is a 1.2 m × 0.9 m
    rectangle topped by a semicircle of diameter 0.9 m (radius 0.45). Rectangle: 1.08 m².
    Semicircle: ½ × π × 0.45² ≈ 0.318 m². Total glazing ≈ 1.398 ≈ 1.40 m²; its perimeter
    is two sides (1.2 + 0.9) plus the semicircular arc (π × 0.45 ≈ 1.41) ≈ 3.51 m — and
    note the frame needs the <em>perimeter</em> while the glass quote asks for the
    <em>area</em>: two prices, two measures, one shape. A worked subtraction: a 5 m × 4 m
    lawn with a 2 m × 1 m pond needs (20 − 2) = 18 m² of turf — compute the whole, remove
    the part, and keep the units squared throughout.
  </p>

  <h2>The square–cube law: where the formulas collide</h2>
  <p>
    Now drag the slider with intent. Perimeter scales as a, area as a², volume as a³ — so as
    anything grows, volume races ahead of surface. The ratio readout, volume per unit of
    surface, grows linearly with size, and that single line explains a surprising amount of
    the world. <strong>Strength scales with area, weight with volume:</strong> a mouse falls
    off a table and bounces; a horse falls and breaks bones — Galileo worked this out in 1638,
    and it is why elephants have pillar legs while ants lift fifty times their weight.
    <strong>Heat loss scales with surface, heat storage with volume:</strong> big animals stay
    warm and small ones must eat almost continuously to hold temperature; bergmann's-rule
    populations tend larger in cold climates. <strong>Cells are small</strong> because
    diffusion through a surface cannot feed a volume that grows too fast, and chips have
    cooling problems for the same reason. Even recipes don't scale linearly: doubling a dish
    doubles the volume but the pan area — where browning happens — grows less, so cooking
    times stretch.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The deep structure behind "length, area, volume" is <strong>dimensional analysis</strong>:
    every physical quantity carries units, and formulas are only valid when the dimensions
    balance. Perimeter formulas are linear in length; area formulas quadratic; volume cubic.
    Test any claimed formula by its dimensions and half of all errors evaporate — if someone
    proposes area = 2πr for a disc, the units alone (metres, not metres²) refute it, no
    measurement needed. The technique, formalised in the 19th century, extends far beyond
    geometry: it is how engineers check equations and how physicists derive scaling laws
    (terminal velocity ∝ √(mass/drag area), for instance) without solving anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two final refinements. First, π is <strong>irrational</strong> (proven by Lambert, 1761)
    and <strong>transcendental</strong> (Lindemann, 1882) — no fraction of integers, and no
    root of any integer polynomial. The transcendence closes an ancient question: since
    constructible numbers are algebraic, squaring the circle with compass and straightedge is
    impossible, full stop. Second, for irregular shapes the exact formulas give way to
    integration — the area under a curve, the volume of a solid of revolution — and to
    numerical estimation: count the squares a shape covers, or use <strong>Cavalieri's
    principle</strong> (solids with equal cross-sectional areas at every height have equal
    volumes), which is precisely why the cone is ⅓ of its cylinder and why the hemisphere
    trick works. Geometry's formulas, it turns out, are just the closed-form answers to
    "how many unit squares?" — and when the answer has no closed form, the counting
    goes on.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galileo, Discourses (1638), Two New Sciences; Archimedes, On the Sphere and Cylinder; OpenStax, Prealgebra (measurement units); Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics syllabus (E5).</p>
</TopicPage>
